```

## REST endpoint

### Registration

Namespace: `selecta/v1`
Route: `GET /wp-json/selecta/v1/search`
Permission callback: `__return_true` (public read, no auth required)
Register on: `rest_api_init`

### Query parameter `q`

- Required.
- Sanitize with `sanitize_text_field()` before any use.
- If missing, empty, or fewer than 3 characters: return empty results array immediately, do not run a database query.
- Length check must use `mb_strlen( trim( $query ) ) < 3`. Do not use `strlen()` — Bulgarian/Cyrillic characters are multi-byte and `strlen()` counts bytes, not characters. A 3-character Cyrillic word is 6 bytes in UTF-8 and would fail a `strlen() < 3` check incorrectly.

### WP_Query rules

- `post_type`: `selecta_product`
- `post_status`: `publish`
- `posts_per_page`: `10` — hard limit, never higher
- `no_found_rows`: `true` — skip total count for performance
- `update_post_meta_cache`: `false`
- `update_post_term_cache`: `false`
- Use a `meta_query` against `_selecta_search_index` with a `LIKE` comparison. Build the value as `'%' . $wpdb->esc_like( $sanitized_query ) . '%'` — never place the raw query string directly inside the wildcards. `$wpdb->esc_like()` escapes any literal `%` or `_` characters in the user's input so they are treated as plain text, not SQL LIKE wildcards. Use `CHAR` cast.
- Also add an `OR` clause matching the post title with the same `$wpdb->esc_like()` pattern, so exact title matches always surface even before the index is built for a post. Use a `posts_clauses` filter or a secondary query pass — keep it simple.
- After the query, call `wp_reset_postdata()`.

### Response shape

Return a JSON array. Each element:

```json
{
  "id": 123,
  "title": "Product title",
  "url": "https://example.com/products/product-slug/",
  "line": "Boucles",
  "benefit": "Хидратира и дефинира",
  "image_url": "https://example.com/wp-content/uploads/...",
  "image_alt": "Product title"
}
```

- `line`: first term name from `selecta_product_line`, or empty string.
- `benefit`: value of `product_short_benefits` ACF field, first line only (split on newline, take index 0), or empty string.
- `image_url` / `image_alt`: from `wp_get_attachment_image_src()` on the featured image at `medium` size. If no featured image, `image_url` is empty string.
- Return an empty array `[]` when there are no results, never `null` or `false`.
- Do not include `product_price`, `product_score`, `product_badge`, or any commerce or admin-only field in the response.

## Search index meta field

### Key

`_selecta_search_index`

### What it is

A single plain-text string per product. Concatenation of all searchable fields with spaces between them. Used by the REST endpoint's `meta_query`.

### Builder function

Name: `selecta_build_search_index( $post_id ): void`

Do not add a strict `int` type hint on `$post_id`. The ACF `acf/save_post` hook may pass the post ID as a string. A strict scalar type hint would throw a `TypeError` in that case. Cast to int inside the function where needed: `$post_id = (int) $post_id;`.

Location: `inc/search.php`

#### Guards — return early if any of these are true

- `defined( 'DOING_AUTOSAVE' ) && DOING_AUTOSAVE`
- `wp_is_post_revision( $post_id )`
- `get_post_type( $post_id ) !== 'selecta_product'`
- `get_post_status( $post_id ) === 'trash'`

#### Fields to index — in this order

The order below is the concatenation order inside the index string. It does NOT create search ranking or scoring. The REST endpoint uses a `LIKE` query which matches anywhere in the string with equal weight regardless of position. The order is documented here only so the index is built consistently and can be read predictably in the database.

| Order | Source | How to get it |
|-------|--------|---------------|
| 1 | Post title | `get_the_title( $post_id )` |
| 2 | `selecta_product_line` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_product_line', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 3 | `selecta_product_category` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_product_category', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 4 | `selecta_hair_concern` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_hair_concern', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 5 | `selecta_hair_type` terms | `wp_get_object_terms( $post_id, 'selecta_hair_type', ['fields' => 'names'] )` |
| 6 | `product_short_benefits` | `get_field( 'product_short_benefits', $post_id )` — ACF textarea |
| 7 | `product_badge` | `get_field( 'product_badge', $post_id )` — ACF text |
| 8 | `product_sections` body_text | Loop `have_rows( 'product_sections', $post_id )`. For each row where `get_row_layout() === 'product_description'`, get `get_sub_field( 'body_text' )` and strip HTML with `wp_strip_all_tags()`. |

#### Fields explicitly excluded from the index

- `product_price` — commerce, must never be indexed
- `product_score` — presentational only
- `product_hover_image` — binary asset reference
- Any ACF image or file field
- Post date, post author, slug

#### Build steps

1. Collect all values into a flat PHP array of strings.
2. For each value: check it is a non-empty string. Skip `WP_Error` returns from taxonomy queries.
3. For any value containing HTML: run `wp_strip_all_tags()` before adding to the array.
4. Implode with a single space: `implode( ' ', array_filter( $parts ) )`.
5. Save: `update_post_meta( $post_id, '_selecta_search_index', sanitize_textarea_field( $index_string ) )`.

#### Hooks to trigger the builder

Hook 1 — native WordPress save:
```
add_action( 'save_post_selecta_product', 'selecta_build_search_index', 20 );
```

Hook 2 — ACF save (covers field-group saves that bypass `save_post`):
```
add_action( 'acf/save_post', 'selecta_build_search_index', 20 );
```
Guard inside the function handles the post-type check so the ACF hook is safe to fire broadly.

#### Future excerpt support

The CPT currently supports `title`, `thumbnail`, `revisions` only — no `editor`, no `excerpt`.
If `excerpt` support is added to the CPT later, add `get_the_excerpt( $post_id )` to the index builder at priority Medium (between `product_short_benefits` and `product_badge`).
No other change is needed.

## JavaScript behaviour

Add a self-contained search module to `assets/js/main.js`.
Append after all existing code. Do not rewrite or restructure the existing file.
Use vanilla JS only. No jQuery, no fetch polyfill (modern browsers only).

### Constants — read from `window.selectaSearch` (localized)

```
MIN_CHARS    = 3
DEBOUNCE_MS  = 250
REST_URL     = window.selectaSearch.restUrl
```

### Overlay open/close

- Search button click: open overlay, set `aria-expanded="true"` on button, remove `hidden` from overlay, focus the search input.
- Close button click: close overlay, set `aria-expanded="false"` on button, add `hidden` to overlay, return focus to search button.
- `Escape` key while overlay is open: same as close button.
- Click on the backdrop (outside overlay content): close overlay.

### Input handling

- Listen to `input` event on the search field.
- Debounce: wait `DEBOUNCE_MS` (250ms) after the last keystroke before acting.
- If trimmed value is fewer than `MIN_CHARS` (3) characters: clear results list, hide loading and empty states. Do not fetch.
- If trimmed value is `MIN_CHARS` or more: fetch from `REST_URL`.

### Fetch

- `GET` request: `REST_URL + '?q=' + encodeURIComponent( trimmedQuery )`
- Show loading state before fetch.
- On success: hide loading state, render results or empty state.
- On error: hide loading state. Do not surface the error message to the user.
- Ignore responses where the query has already changed (stale response guard: compare query string before rendering).

### Render

- Clear the results `<ul>` before rendering.
- Max 10 items (server enforces this; JS renders whatever the server returns, up to 10).
- Each result: `<li role="option">` wrapping `<a href="{url}">` containing:
  - Image `<img>` or placeholder `<span>` if no image.
  - Line label `<span>` — hidden if empty.
  - Title `<span>`.
  - Benefit `<span>` — hidden if empty.
- Clicking a result closes the overlay.
- No price. No rating. No add-to-cart. No badge in results.
- Never use `innerHTML` to render any value that comes from the REST API response (title, line, benefit, URL, image alt, etc.). These values originate from user-entered WordPress content and are an XSS vector if injected via `innerHTML`. Use `textContent` to set text values. Use `setAttribute` or direct property assignment for `href`, `src`, `alt`. Create elements with `document.createElement()` and build the result node programmatically.

### ARIA

- `aria-expanded` on the search button reflects open/closed state.
- `aria-busy="true"` on the results container while a fetch is pending.
- `aria-busy` removed when fetch completes (success or error).
- Overlay has `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, `aria-label` pointing to the overlay heading or a descriptive label.

### Focus trap

- While the overlay is open, Tab and Shift+Tab must cycle between: search input, result links, close button.
- Focus must not escape to the page behind the overlay.

### Reduced motion

- If `window.matchMedia( '(prefers-reduced-motion: reduce)' ).matches` is true, skip any CSS transition toggling.

## PHP template: search-overlay.php

File: `template-parts/components/search-overlay.php`

Include once in `header.php`, just before `</header>`.

Required elements:
- Outer: `<div id="search-overlay" class="search-overlay" role="dialog" aria-modal="true" aria-label="..." hidden>`
- Search input: `type="search"`, `id="search-input"`, `autocomplete="off"`, `spellcheck="false"`
- Close button: `type="button"`, keyboard accessible, focusable
- Results list: `<ul id="search-results" class="search-overlay__results" role="listbox">`
- Loading state: `<p id="search-loading" class="search-overlay__loading" hidden>`
- Empty state: `<p id="search-no-results" class="search-overlay__empty" hidden>`

No product data is hard-coded in this template. All results are rendered by JS.

## Search button in site-header-bar.php

The existing button has class `site-header__action--search`.
Add these two attributes:
- `aria-expanded="false"`
- `aria-controls="search-overlay"`

Do not change anything else in that file.

## wp_localize_script in assets.php

Add inside `selecta_enqueue_assets()` after the main script is enqueued:

```php
wp_localize_script(
    'selecta-main',
    'selectaSearch',
    array(
        'restUrl'    => esc_url_raw( rest_url( 'selecta/v1/search' ) ),
        'minChars'   => 3,
        'debounceMs' => 250,
    )
);
```

## Implementation order

Work one task at a time. Stop and report after each.

```
Step 1  inc/search.php — REST endpoint registration + index builder (no UI)
Step 2  Rebuild index for any existing products (WP-CLI or a one-time admin action)
Step 3  template-parts/components/search-overlay.php — markup only, no JS wired yet
Step 4  site-header-bar.php — add aria attributes; header.php — include template part
Step 5  assets/css/components/search-overlay.css — styles
Step 6  assets/js/main.js — append search JS module
Step 7  inc/assets.php — enqueue CSS + wp_localize_script
Step 8  Keyboard/accessibility pass
Step 9  Mobile responsive pass (later task, not in scope for initial build)
```

## Quality checklist before finishing any search task

- Endpoint returns only `selecta_product` posts, `post_status = publish`.
- Query sanitized with `sanitize_text_field()`.
- Minimum 3 characters enforced server-side — short queries return `[]` immediately.
- Maximum 10 results enforced server-side.
- Response contains no price, stock, cart, rating, or commerce field.
- Index builder guards against autosave, revisions, wrong post type, and trashed posts.
- `wp_reset_postdata()` called after any custom `WP_Query`.
- `_selecta_search_index` stored with `sanitize_textarea_field()`.
- Overlay has `role="dialog"`, `aria-modal="true"`, `hidden` when closed.
- Search button `aria-expanded` is toggled correctly by JS.
- Escape key closes overlay and returns focus to the search button.
- Focus is trapped inside the overlay while open.
- REST URL comes from `window.selectaSearch.restUrl` — never hardcoded.
- JS is appended to the existing `main.js` without rewriting the file.
- CSS is a new component file, not added to `main.css`.
- No jQuery used anywhere in the search feature.
- No search plugin installed.
- No external search service used.
